<td id="S4.T3.7.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.7.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">68.95</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Steering partially redistributes sensitivity, reducing KL divergence but not fully eliminating the bottleneck. In contrast, Stable-Mamba exhibits more uniform sensitivity and substantially reduced KL at Layer 20, suggesting effective mitigation of this compression point.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Quantitatively identifying Activation Subspace Bottlenecks</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having motivated the search for useful subspaces in SSMs, we now quantitatively search for activation bottlenecks that may be useful for steering.
To do so, we first employ SPD, which provide layer-wise, parameter-level metrics (see description in <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS2" title="3.1.2 Quantitatively identifying Activation Subspace Bottlenecks ‣ 3.1 Identifying Activation Subspace Bottlenecks ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Interpreting and Steering State-Space Modelsvia Activation Subspace Bottlenecks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="S4.T4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Layer-level functional roles based on SPD statistics.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T4.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Phase</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Layers</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Entropy</span></td>
